================================================================================
TRANSITION PHASE: SKILLS -> CONTACT (The Ascent & The Wash)

Vibe: Visceral, physical, and atmospheric. Escaping the digital ocean, walking
onto a desolate night beach, and discovering an ancient artifact.
Goal: Execute a flawless transition from 2D horizontal scrolling to a 1st-person
3D "walking" camera, culminating in a complex WebGL fluid masking reveal.

STAGE 1: THE ASCENT (Breaking the Surface)

This occurs at the extreme right edge of the Skills section timeline (the end
of the 300vw horizontal scroll).

The Camera Pitch:

Horizontal panning stops.

As the user continues to scroll, the camera executes a simulated 3D "pitch"
upwards (looking towards the surface).

The bioluminescent #0A1128 water gradients fade to a lighter, murky teal
as we approach the "surface".

The Breach:

The moment the scroll threshold is crossed to enter the Contact section,
a full-screen "Water Droplet / Lens Clear" WebGL displacement effect triggers.

The screen flashes a muted cyan/white for 0.2 seconds, simulating the
camera breaking through the water tension into the air.

Palette Swap: The background instantly becomes #000000 (Pitch Black night sky).

STAGE 2: THE BEACH WALK (1st Person Camera Mechanics)

Standard vertical scrolling resumes, but the mapping of that scroll is
fundamentally altered to mimic human locomotion.

The Environment:

A subtle, very dark, grainy texture overlays the bottom half of the screen
(wet sand).

Ambient sound (optional, but implied visually): The rhythm of the visuals
matches heavy footsteps in wet sand.

The "Head Bob" Equation (Scroll-Linked Camera):

The user's vertical scroll (scrollY) does NOT move a standard page up/down.
It drives a mathematical timeline moving the camera forward (Z-axis).

Y-Axis Bob: translateY(Math.sin(scrollY * frequency) * amplitude)

Z-Axis Roll: rotateZ(Math.cos(scrollY * frequency) * slight_amplitude)

Effect: As the user scrolls down, the entire viewport slightly dips, rises,
and tilts left/right in a rhythmic pattern, perfectly mimicking a person
walking forward towards a monolith.

STAGE 3: THE DIRT MASK & THE MONOLITH

As the user "walks" forward, a massive, screen-filling rock formation emerges
from the pitch blackness.

The Monolith Textures (Layering):

Layer 1 (Bottom): The Clean Stone. Dark, wet, carved rock featuring your
glowing #FCD34D (Amber) contact links (Resume, GitHub, etc.) deeply
embossed into it.

Layer 2 (Top): The Dirt & Sand mask. A highly textured, opaque layer of
light ochre and gray sand that completely obscures the clean stone and
the contact links underneath.

STAGE 4: THE WASH (The Reveal)

The user reaches the monolith. The "walking" scroll locks, and the final
physics interaction takes over.

The Trigger:

When the camera is directly in front of the rock (scroll locked), a massive
digital "wave" crashes against the screen from the bottom edge.

The Fluid Shader (Erosion Effect):

A WebGL fluid distortion pass sweeps upwards across the rock.

Erase Masking: The wave acts as an alpha-mask eraser. Wherever the "water"
touches Layer 2 (The Dirt), the dirt texture rapidly dissolves and washes
away downwards, governed by a noise map so it clumps and streaks organically
rather than fading as a flat block.

The Aftermath:

The dirt is gone. The underlying "Clean Stone" layer is revealed.

The embossed contact links glow warmly in the dark.

A faint, scrolling normal-map overlay makes the stone look wet and
glistening for a few seconds before drying/settling into the pitch-black
"Cave" environment.

STAGE 5: HAND-OFF TO CAVE FLASHLIGHT

Transition to Darkness:

Once the wave recedes and the links are revealed, the ambient brightness
of the rock slowly fades to 0% over 2 seconds.

The screen is now pure black.

Cursor Re-initialization:

The cursor, which was disabled during the "Walk" and "Wash", reactivates.

It now functions as the Radial Gradient Flashlight, ready for the user to
sweep across the darkness to find the links and the unlit torch.

TECHNICAL UX & PERFORMANCE NOTES

The Walking Camera: Do not overdo the amplitude of the head bob. A maximum
translation of 15-20px Y and 1 degree Z-rotation is enough to sell the effect.
Anything higher will cause severe motion sickness for the user.

The Wash Shader: This MUST be done using WebGL (Three.js custom shader material).
You pass two textures (Dirt and Clean Rock) and use a third animated grayscale
noise texture driven by a uniform u_time or u_progress to mix between
them. CSS masking is not performant enough to simulate realistic liquid erosion.